From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yugidraft <yugidraft@localhost>
Date: Sat, 12 Feb 2000 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] core: bind one opponent at activation for n > 2

Lua "1" means one opponent. Before this change the core guessed it (the next living opponent) and wrote a trap record.
Now the opponent is bound when the effect is activated, and the binding stays for the whole chain link.

Binding order: the event opponent; then a probe at AddChain step 0;
then a lazy prompt in a yieldable cost or target; a context that cannot yield keeps the old fallback.
The probe runs the checks for each living opponent when the first run reads a single opponent. The opponents that pass are the legal set.
One opponent: bound without a prompt. More than one: the activator gets MSG_SELECT_OPTION, every option desc 0xFFFE0000|seat.
An out-of-range answer gives MSG_RETRY from the stock option prompt. The direct attack pick stays 0xFFFF0000|seat.
Summon procedures with a range on the opponent side (Kaiju, Lava Golem) bind in the same way; SpSummonRule asks, and the monster goes to the chosen opponent.
In Tag the pick is between the opponent seats. Eliminated seats are never offered.
The event opponent counts only an event that names a player: the event player of a subject event (damage, draw, chain link, summon, custom), the reason player if the event has a reason. The chain end, the phase events and the idle activation name nobody, so the pick runs.
Team level calls in Tag need no opponent: GetLP, CheckLPCost, SetTargetPlayer and the player of an operation info store or read the team only, so there is no prompt and no mark.
A battle event names the other side of the battle: the controller of the attacked monster for the attacker side, the controller of the attacker for the others (Yubel).
A bound opponent that is eliminated before the link resolves gives the empty result for single-opponent calls, queries and hand or Deck reads of the other side. There is no fallback to another opponent (ADR 0002).
An action call that reads "1" with nothing bound marks the probe, so the pick is asked. The answer of the lazy prompt binds only in the scope of the asking duelist that has a chain link. The pick and the legal set of a summon procedure are forgotten when the procedure ends or the effect is deleted.
With 2 duelists nothing runs: every new path is guarded by n_duelists > 2.
